  {
   "id": "XB2yb0E_6xMJ",
   "from": "Joshua Wiley",
   "author": "065110cca96a6373",
   "date": "2010-10-27T17:00:51Z",
   "subject": "Re: barplot with data on the heights of bars",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi Peng,<p>You are looking for stat = &quot;identity&quot;.  For example:<p># Some data<br>dat &lt;- data.frame(Group = c(&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot;),<br>                  height = c(50, 60, 70))<p># A bar plot<br>ggplot(data = dat, aes(x = Group, y = height)) +<br>  geom_bar(stat = &quot;identity&quot;)<p># Also note that no information is lost if you do<p>ggplot(data = dat, aes(x = Group, y = height)) +<br>  geom_point(size = 3) + scale_y_continuous(lim = c(0, 70))<p>Cheers,<p>Josh<p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<p><p><p><p><p><p><p><p>On Wed, Oct 27, 2010 at 9:51 AM, Peng Yu &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">peng...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br>&gt; On Wed, Oct 27, 2010 at 11:47 AM, Brandon Hurr &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">bhi...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br>&gt;&gt; Peng,<br>&gt;&gt; I get bars with your code.<br>&gt;&gt; Brandon<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Of course you will. But you misunderstood me.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; I&#39;m asking if I have a vector(see below), how to plot a bar plot with<br>&gt; three bars (A,B,C) each with height 50, 60 and 70.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; c(A=50,B=60,C=70)<br>&gt;<br>&gt;&gt; On Wed, Oct 27, 2010 at 17:40, Peng Yu &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">peng...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br>&gt;&gt;&gt;<br>&gt;&gt;&gt; qplot(color, data=diamonds, geom=&#39;bar&#39;)<br>&gt;&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; --<br>&gt; Regards,<br>&gt; Peng<br>&gt;<br></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p>"
    ],
